===== path p track init
Start a tracking session; reads initial content from stdin

Usage: path p track init [OPTIONS] --file <FILE> --actor <ACTOR>

Options:
      --file <FILE>
          Artifact path (used as the change-map key)

      --actor <ACTOR>
          Default actor (e.g. "human:alex")

      --actor-def <ACTOR_DEF>
          Actor definition as JSON (e.g. '{"human:alex": {"name": "Alex"}}')

      --title <TITLE>
          Path title (appears in meta.title)

      --source <SOURCE>
          Path source tag (appears in meta.source)

      --base-uri <BASE_URI>
          Base URI for the path (e.g. "github:org/repo")

      --base-ref <BASE_REF>
          Base ref (e.g. commit hash)

      --base-branch <BASE_BRANCH>
          Branch the path was opened against (appears in path.base.branch)

      --id <ID>
          Path and graph id (default: a fresh `track-<time>-<pid>`); also names the session file, so a fixed id makes the output reproducible

      --session-dir <SESSION_DIR>
          Directory for session state files (default: $TMPDIR)

      --pretty
          Pretty-print JSON output

      --picker <PICKER>
          Backend for the interactive fuzzy picker used by `share`, `resume`, and `p import <provider>`. `auto` (default) uses the embedded skim picker and falls back to external `fzf` only when skim isn't compiled in; a hint is printed if `fzf` is also on PATH. `fzf`/`skim` force one backend and error if it isn't available

          Possible values:
          - auto: Pick whichever backend is available; prefer the embedded skim picker and fall back to external `fzf` when skim isn't compiled in. When external `fzf` is also on `PATH`, a one-time hint is printed to stderr advising `--picker fzf` for users who want their fzf config / keybindings
          - fzf:  Force the external `fzf` binary. Errors if it isn't on `PATH`
          - skim: Force the embedded skim picker. Errors if path-cli was built with `--no-default-features`
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
